WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.490
This is Andy from 1995's "Toy Story."

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.710
Showing off a 3D-animated human

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:09.300
was a groundbreaking
achievement at the time,

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.750
but he clearly lacks details
when compared to someone

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:16.770
like Dash from 2018's "Incredibles 2."

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.100
His freckles are way more prominent,

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.430
his long hair is less stiff,

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:24.290
and his expressions are more noticeable.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:28.630
Humans have always been a
challenging obstacle for Pixar,

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.140
and getting from 1995's Andy

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.870
to where we are today
took its animators years

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.320
and countless simulations
to master all the elements

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:40.478
that make up a person:

00:00:40.478 --> 00:00:41.661
hair,

00:00:41.661 --> 00:00:42.744
skin,

00:00:42.744 --> 00:00:43.577
[Mr. Incredible grunting]

00:00:43.577 --> 00:00:44.726
muscles,

00:00:44.726 --> 00:00:45.752
and movement.

00:00:45.752 --> 00:00:48.240
[Carl groaning]

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.550
When Pixar embarked on its
first feature-length film,

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.845
it still had work to do to figure out

00:00:53.845 --> 00:00:57.245
how to make 3D-animated human characters.

00:00:57.245 --> 00:00:59.128
When animators tried,

00:00:59.128 --> 00:01:02.161
some of their digital
models came out stiff,

00:01:02.161 --> 00:01:03.575
so they came up with some

00:01:03.575 --> 00:01:06.110
creative solutions where they could.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.820
Lighting was one clever way
to hide those imperfections,

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:12.480
shrouding human characters in darkness,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.970
like in this scene at Pizza Planet.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.060
But in order to avoid
showing bodies completely,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.080
they staged many shots so you
could only see feet and hands,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.660
like this shot of Andy's
mom walking in the room.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.002
Creating a variety of different people

00:01:28.002 --> 00:01:29.693
of all shapes and sizes,

00:01:29.693 --> 00:01:32.168
and especially the clothes
that they would wear,

00:01:32.168 --> 00:01:34.570
was especially time consuming.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.550
So instead of customizing
every individual,

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.366
they Frankensteined it,

00:01:39.366 --> 00:01:41.774
copying body parts from some humans

00:01:41.774 --> 00:01:44.620
and creating new humans
out of those parts,

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.600
while changing little bits and pieces

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:48.520
of their physical appearance.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.700
But even in that first feature film,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.470
they did have an early breakthrough.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.583
Animators needed to distinguish between

00:01:55.583 --> 00:01:58.770
the look of the toys and
the look of the humans.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.510
One way to do this was through movement.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.510
Head animator Pete Docter
observed, for example,

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.810
that Woody was floppy and loose.

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.120
Therefore, he would
have quicker movements.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.430
On the other hand, humans
were bigger and heavier,

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:16.000
meaning they had to slow
their movements down.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.250
1997 "Geri's Game" was
the first Pixar project

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.170
to feature a human character as its lead.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:24.970
This four-and-a-half-minute short

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:26.960
was basically an experiment.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.772
One of the most prominent features

00:02:28.772 --> 00:02:31.710
to focus on for Geri was his skin.

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.150
Human skin needs to be smooth.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:37.900
Prior to "Geri's Game," human
models were built in patches.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:41.150
Skin sometimes came out
looking more plastic.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.870
This is where math came to the rescue.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:46.030
Enter the subdivision technique,

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:49.540
which is when an animator
subdivides a hard-edged shape

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.400
enough times that it becomes
a soft and smooth surface.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.710
Thanks to subdivision, Geri's
hand looks much smoother,

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.030
like a human hand should.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.030
This method has been used
on every Pixar movie since,

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.670
smoothing out everything from
humans to bugs to buildings.

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:11.800
That work allowed Andy and the
rest of the human characters

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.066
to spend less time in the shadows

00:03:14.066 --> 00:03:17.150
in 1999's "Toy Story 2."

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.600
Another tool that pulled
humans out of the shadows

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:22.740
was a RenderMan shader.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.090
This let animators paint on details

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:26.820
to the basic character renderings

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.790
like pores, veins, and blotches.

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.270
There is no better example of this advance

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.290
than toy collector Al.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.050
You can see the skin
imperfections on his face

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.560
and even a 5 o'clock shadow.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:43.140
And this shader tool would
be used all the time.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.281
It's how they made sure Mamá Coco

00:03:45.281 --> 00:03:47.781
had such distinct facial details

00:03:47.781 --> 00:03:50.330
compared to other characters in "Coco,"

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:53.650
but we didn't see that until 2017.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:55.840
At the precipice of the new millennium,

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.090
Pixar was inching closer
to giving animated humans

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.226
even more screen time.

00:04:01.226 --> 00:04:02.059
[television crashes]

00:04:02.059 --> 00:04:03.074
Boo: Uh-oh.

00:04:03.074 --> 00:04:04.010
[Mike and Sulley screaming]

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:06.380
Narrator: There were still
a few hoops to jump through,

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.910
which you can see in
2001's "Monsters, Inc."

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.500
One of the biggest issues
with human characters

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.895
continued to be their clothes.

00:04:14.895 --> 00:04:17.750
Boo, the movie's only human character,

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:19.780
wears a big flowy shirt.

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.810
Prior to "Monsters, Inc.,"
characters wore clothes

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:25.120
that moved automatically with them.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:28.332
A new simulator called Fizt enabled them

00:04:28.332 --> 00:04:31.380
to animate Boo and her shirt separately.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.030
Look at how much more natural

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:35.105
the movement of Boo's shirt looks

00:04:35.105 --> 00:04:36.970
compared to Sid's sister's shirt

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:38.950
in this moment from "Toy Story,"

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:42.040
where the shirt barely moves as she does.

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.140
And while Fizt allowed
for both better clothing

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:47.530
and all the fur you see on Sulley,

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:49.410
it couldn't do everything.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:53.270
Director Pete Docter wanted
to give Boo long hair,

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:56.240
but to do that, the animators
would have had to animate

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.860
in pieces instead of in one block.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:02.440
So to save them the trouble,
they gave her pigtails instead,

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.710
which were easier to manage.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.730
Now that Pixar could create
skin and make clothes move,

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.020
it was time to make a feature-length movie

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.037
entirely populated by human characters

00:05:14.037 --> 00:05:17.570
in the form of 2004's "The Incredibles."

00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:19.360
One of the first tasks?

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.480
Conquering long hair.

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:25.640
Director Brad Bird insisted
Violet have the long hair

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.620
that Boo didn't have.

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:30.760
Animators already had to deal
with a lot of hair collisions

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:32.120
on a normal head of hair,

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.230
so you can imagine how much
they dealt with it for Violet,

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:37.540
especially with how many
different situations

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:39.150
she finds herself in

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.800
and all the times she plays with her hair.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.680
The team developed a
core simulation engine

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:47.720
that handled all the
collisions caused by long hair

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.290
while keeping it moving and flowing,

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:53.320
as well as the tools that
gave animators more control

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:56.300
over the placement and
velocity of her hair.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.160
Figuring out how to properly
animate and then render

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.300
Violet's signature hair
took six months of work,

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.270
but Violet wasn't the only
character pushing the way

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.850
Pixar physically builds humans.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:11.250
Mr. Incredible is all muscle,

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:14.380
and none of Pixar's human
characters resembled him.

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:18.150
For that reason, animators
used a software called Goo,

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.090
which allows the skin to react

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:22.260
to moving and sliding muscles,

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:26.200
making a character's movement
look a lot more natural.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:27.950
While many of the developments created

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:29.533
for the making of "The Incredibles"

00:06:29.533 --> 00:06:31.620
were specific for certain characters,

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:35.950
the studio wasn't done perfecting
the look of human skin.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:40.130
See, human skin is actually
somewhat transparent.

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:43.750
Some light needs to go through
the skin, scatter around,

00:06:43.750 --> 00:06:45.630
and then reflect back.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:48.990
If the skin looks too
solid and no light gets in,

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:50.750
it won't look natural.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:55.140
So animators used subsurface
scattering to render skin.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:57.620
With subsurface scattering applied,

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.220
skin no longer just looked
like a rigid surface.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.610
They actually found
inspiration for the skin

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:05.600
in an unlikely place.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.070
The tools used to make
the "Finding Nemo" fish

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.810
look translucent were
used to scatter light

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.110
off the skin of humans here.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:16.290
But with these advancements,
the studio never forgot

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:19.000
that the skin couldn't look too realistic,

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.140
despite the capabilities that the shader

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:24.040
and the subsurface scattering now allowed.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.290
If a human were to look too real,

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:28.050
then they would enter into the realm

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:30.840
of the disturbing uncanny valley.

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:33.580
So while details like follicles and pores

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:36.850
could have been added, they
were purposely left out.

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:39.630
Pixar has been able to
build convincing humans

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.810
that don't follow all the
basic rules of human anatomy

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:44.430
throughout the years.

00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:48.950
For example, Carl
Fredricksen from 2009's "Up."

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:51.150
His head was deliberately designed

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:53.680
to have massive, boxy proportions,

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.630
and instead of adding
every tiny skin detail,

00:07:56.630 --> 00:07:59.130
they focused on the most defining wrinkles

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.100
in an old man's face.

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:04.690
Skin and bone aren't the only
things that make up a human.

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:08.850
Pixar constantly strived to
make humans more expressive.

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.090
By the time "Ratatouille"
was in the works,

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:15.970
Pixar would have 150 controls
just on a character's face.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:18.940
These controls allowed for
the varying movement speeds

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.340
between toys and humans in "Toy Story"

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:24.430
and got even more
sophisticated over the years,

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:27.090
granting animators the
ability to slow down

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:29.870
and minimize the movements
of an elderly character

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:31.980
like Carl in "Up."

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:35.320
Control points also allow
for the complex expressions

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:37.470
of a character like Chef Skinner,

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:40.610
as well as more subtle
and varied body motions,

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:43.420
like Linguini's slapstick movements.

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:47.800
Meanwhile, Violet's long hair
paved the way for Colette,

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:52.770
who had 176,030 hairs on her head.

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:55.263
Advanced simulations had to be applied

00:08:55.263 --> 00:08:58.840
to make sure strands would
not merge into each other.

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:01.670
Despite the sophistication
of Colette's hair,

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:04.140
Pixar still had hair challenges,

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:07.410
specifically mastering curly hair.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:10.930
According to Pixar's chief
technology officer, Steve May,

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:12.840
curly hair needs to be soft

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:15.240
while still holding a curly shape.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:19.120
So in 2009, simulation
supervisor Claudia Chung

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.653
and her team developed a new simulator

00:09:21.653 --> 00:09:24.961
called Taz for 2012's "Brave,"

00:09:24.961 --> 00:09:28.280
which built Merida's
hair as coil-like locks

00:09:28.280 --> 00:09:31.170
to imitate the movement
of real curly hair.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:33.650
But the curls would completely unwind,

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:36.650
so the animators added
invisible core springs

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:38.515
underneath them, allowing the locks

00:09:38.515 --> 00:09:41.030
to move with more control.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:44.400
The better Pixar's animators
got at creating humans,

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:47.650
the easier it was for them to
play around with their form

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:50.120
and literally deconstruct them.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:54.070
2017's "Coco" features a
cast of skeleton characters

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:56.160
that were based on humans.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:58.540
Animators were able to use the same rigs

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:01.120
they would use for humans
covered in flesh and muscle,

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.988
but with some changes, as
the bones needed to move

00:10:03.988 --> 00:10:05.996
in ways that you wouldn't normally see

00:10:05.996 --> 00:10:07.446
in human characters.

00:10:07.446 --> 00:10:11.380
For one, the skeletons were
able to detach their bones,

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:14.090
so the rigs were altered
to allow for this.

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:17.700
A tool called Kingpin made
the bones appear looser

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:21.200
by adding jiggle to them,
which you can see here.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:24.210
Pixar then put all the
skin and muscle back on

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:25.730
for "Incredibles 2,"

00:10:25.730 --> 00:10:28.810
a sequel 14 years in the making.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:31.890
Even with all the
strides made back in 2004

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:33.510
with the first "Incredibles,"

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:36.360
there was still so much
more that Brad Bird

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:39.340
and the animation team
wanted to improve on.

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:40.950
When the first movie came out,

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:44.130
they didn't have the ability
to get every single detail

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:47.303
from the original clay
sculpts of the characters.

00:10:47.303 --> 00:10:48.970
Now they could.

00:10:48.970 --> 00:10:51.840
Just look at the difference
between Jack-Jack

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:53.490
in the two movies.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:56.660
This is partially because
for the first time ever,

00:10:56.660 --> 00:10:59.270
Pixar was able to base
the characters' eyes

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:01.410
off real human eyes.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:04.430
Sure, the eyes are still
bigger and more stylized

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:07.670
than normal, but just
look at this shot of Dash.

00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:09.480
The eyes are less circular

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:12.235
and more of an oval when opened wide.

00:11:12.235 --> 00:11:15.150
Meanwhile, creating and
rendering Violet's long hair

00:11:15.150 --> 00:11:18.110
was easier now than it was in 2004,

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.980
and even more free-flowing here.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:22.570
And speaking of hair,

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:25.010
there were further hair breakthroughs.

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:27.790
In the past, animators
would only be able to work

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:30.360
with scant guide strands of hairs,

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.620
just guessing what the
finalized hair would look like.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:36.190
That would have made it
tough to animate Elastigirl

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:39.730
on her motorcycle as her
hair flows in the wind,

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:42.250
or Violet blow-drying her hair.

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:45.070
But now, animators had full access

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:46.980
to what these characters'
hair would look like

00:11:46.980 --> 00:11:49.890
at every stage of
production before rendering,

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:52.460
allowing them to sculpt better hairstyles

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:55.550
and even catch mistakes early on.

00:11:55.550 --> 00:11:57.287
Another subtle difference between

00:11:57.287 --> 00:11:59.040
the two "Incredibles" movies:

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.460
in "Incredibles 2," the city streets

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:03.410
are more heavily populated.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:06.350
According to producer
Nicole Paradis Grindle,

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:08.192
Bird thought the urban backdrops

00:12:08.192 --> 00:12:10.920
looked too sparse during major moments.

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:14.410
Crowds are hard, as you
have to animate, simulate,

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:17.100
and render a lot of individual people.

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:19.670
So the crowds team actually started using

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.380
some motion capture.

00:12:21.380 --> 00:12:24.508
It was used in "Coco" on
some background skeletons

00:12:24.508 --> 00:12:26.340
and made way for better filming

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:29.350
of background humans in "Incredibles 2."

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:33.270
You can really see just how
far human animation had come

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.720
by the time "Toy Story 4" rolled around

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:38.070
just by looking in the background.

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:41.280
Bonnie's kindergarten class
is more fully realized

00:12:41.280 --> 00:12:44.630
than the dark Pizza Planet
or even Andy's birthday party

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:46.050
from the first movie.

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:48.210
You can see characters with long hair,

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.900
and even in this shot
from a toy's perspective,

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:53.850
all the human faces are clearly seen.

00:12:53.850 --> 00:12:56.270
And they continued to
successfully experiment

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:58.460
with motion capture in order to populate

00:12:58.460 --> 00:13:00.180
the film's carnival scenes.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:02.310
What difference does mo-cap make?

00:13:02.310 --> 00:13:06.050
Well, according to "Toy Story
4" crowds lead Neil Helm,

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:08.940
this technology can make
human background characters

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:11.460
more convincing and detailed.

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:14.850
Crowd characters tend to
run on looping cycles.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:16.925
Mo-cap lets filmmakers capture

00:13:16.925 --> 00:13:19.416
much longer clips of background characters

00:13:19.416 --> 00:13:21.970
carrying out distinct actions.

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:23.928
From subdivision surfaces

00:13:23.928 --> 00:13:26.053
to hair-bending simulations

00:13:26.053 --> 00:13:29.470
to breakthroughs in cloth
and realistic crowds,

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:31.751
mastering the elements of human beings

00:13:31.751 --> 00:13:34.460
allowed Pixar to populate its worlds

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:38.180
and expand the kinds of
stories it was able to tell.

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:40.802
And, most importantly, animators know

00:13:40.802 --> 00:13:44.280
when to give characters enough
detail that they seem human,

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:46.910
but enough exaggeration in their form

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:50.200
that they're still part
of an animated universe.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:52.041
Pixar's next chance to show us

00:13:52.041 --> 00:13:55.041
all the hard work it
does on human characters:

00:13:55.041 --> 00:13:56.743
the upcoming "Soul."

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:01.740
Ian Phillips: Hi! Thanks
so much for watching.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:04.792
If you'd like to see more
videos just like this one,

00:14:04.792 --> 00:14:07.050
remember to hit the subscribe button.

00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:09.380
What other aspects of
Pixar's animation process

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:11.320
do you wanna learn more about?

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:12.753
Let us know in the comments.

